window.docContents[117].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Pull":{"contents":"The oscillator is one body on an ideal, massless spring fixed to an infinitely massive wall, moving along a line. The body is homogeneous, rigid, and point-like; those assumptions are revisited at L3. Its bare-real model has no type-level record of what mass and stiffness mean and accepts the reciprocal-frequency expression √(m/k) where √(k/m) was intended. A dimension tag can catch dimensional mistakes but cannot distinguish kinds that share a dimension. The system context comes before the attributes it needs.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box","header":"5.1.3. Pull","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Pull"},"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/Where-the-calculus-is-left-___-the-boundary-family/#boundary-family":{"contents":"A kind crossing must be authored. The calculus will not infer that two kinds may meet, and the\naudit will not accept that they did without being told. That is the whole design: the set of\nauthored crossings is a closed, enumerable statement of where the type discipline is deliberately\nset aside, in the way #print axioms is a closed statement of what a proof rests on.\n\nThe audit detects a boundary structurally, not textually. A grep cannot see through ⟨…⟩\nconstructor sugar, structure-eta projections, or the match_N and _proof_N auxiliaries Lean lifts\nout of a definition — and it cannot tell a parity theorem about .magnitude (legitimate, a\nstatement in Prop) from a compute body that exits the calculus (a defect). #kind_boundary_audit\nwalks the environment instead, and reports every declaration that constructs or projects a\nregistered carrier.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes","header":"33.3. Where the calculus is left — the boundary family","id":"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/Where-the-calculus-is-left-___-the-boundary-family/#boundary-family"}});